   202	### 3.3 List all the unclear spots before resolving any
   203	
   204	For each spot record:
   205	
   206	| | |
   207	|---|---|
   208	| Kind | missing **information** → a research piece · a **decision** → ask EJ · **unknown** → a bounded explore loop |
   209	| Blocks | which steps cannot start until it is resolved; everything else can start |
   210	| Depends on | another spot whose answer might remove or change this one |
   211	
   212	Questions for EJ go out as one batch, each with a provisional assumption so it can be answered in a few words.
   213	In a prompt file a provisional assumption stands unless EJ corrects it; a workflow design's questions are answered
   214	before the designed workflow runs, except blueprint questions, which hold back only the pieces that build on them.
   215	Blueprint questions are the exception in a prompt file too: they need an explicit
   216	answer before any step that builds on them runs (3.1, blueprint check). Where spots are resolved separately, one
   217	joining step compares the answers. A contradiction is a stop: pick one and say why, never average.
   218	
   219	### 3.4 Size
   220	
   221	- Up to 3 steps and nothing unclear except decisions that have a provisional assumption → one piece → a prompt
   222	  file, with those decisions listed at the top as questions for EJ. A spot of kind information or unknown always
   223	  means a workflow design. (Changed 2026-09-20 after trial 1, where three decisions with obvious defaults helped
   224	  push a one-line fix out of "small"; n=1.) Blueprint spots follow the same rule: a missing or incomplete section
   225	  always means a workflow design; a draft section alone does not.
   226	- More than 3 steps → consider a new piece. Split only if each part keeps its own check **and** splitting changes
   227	  how the work is done ("Split iff it changes the route", `challenge-mediation` skill).
   228	- Risk is judged separately from size: how late a mistake would be noticed, whether it can be undone, how many
   229	  things it touches. Tiny but risky → the prompt file gets one independent check.
   230	
   231	**Default-first design.** When the size decision says workflow design, the design starts from a lookup, not a
   232	blank page: each piece's category row (labelled in 3.2) or combination pipeline in `docs/TASK_TYPES.md` supplies the default pattern,
   233	engine, check and sabotage; `others` gets the full method from first principles. A bespoke (challenger) design
   234	replaces a default only on a written claim of ≥20% lower projected run cost **at equal coverage** (same
   235	criteria ids, same checks, same blindness — coverage is a gate, never an axis). The decision is reviewed by the
   236	script gate (`design_gate.py`) plus one blind fixed-checklist pass (Sonnet 5.5; a different kind when the
   237	design builds or EJ flagged high risk), and every run reconciles its claim in `docs/TASK_TYPES_LEDGER.md` —
   238	that ledger is how the defaults earn their n. (Added 2026-10-01 at EJ's request; n=0.)
   239	
   240	Anchors for the number 3: Gate splits capability lists over three (`ancient_games/stages.py:109`) and the
   241	the plan-wide cap of 3 dispatched corroborating sources (`ancient_games/registry.py:24`, used at `stages.py:309`; it caps dispatches per plan, not agents running at once — corrected 2026-10-03).
   242	
   243	### 3.5 Clear pieces run as loops
   244	
   245	While the unclear spots are being resolved, the pieces that are already clear and not blocked run as loops: a
   246	defined output, the agent attempts, the check runs, pass → close the loop, fail → the feedback goes back to the agent.
   247	
   248	A piece is loop-ready only if it has all five:
   249	
   250	1. A check. Best is a script; next a fixed checklist judged by a separate agent; last EJ's own judgment.
   251	2. A limit on attempts.
   252	3. Specific feedback: the script's real output, not just "failed".
   253	4. An exit for when the limit is hit. If the attempts ran on a cheap tier, the exit is a fresh node on a stronger
   254	   tier with a short handoff note (the piece, its check, the last feedback) and its own limit. When that limit is
   255	   hit too, or there is no stronger tier, the piece goes back to the unclear list or to EJ. It was not as clear as
   256	   it looked. (Tier exit added 2026-09-22 at EJ's request; n=0.)
   257	5. A check the worker cannot edit, and that is known to be able to fail (the house sabotage check).
   258	
   259	A loop only closes when its check is objective. (n=1: a planner/critic loop whose check was "try to reject it" ran
   260	three rounds, 6 → 5 → 4 problems, and never closed. See `20260919-plan-critic-issues.md`.)
   261	
   262	Attempts start on the cheapest tier that could plausibly do the piece; a stronger tier, which costs many times more
   263	per token, is bought only for what the cheap one could not finish. Escalation is a fresh node, never a tier switch
   264	inside the running session: a switch voids the prompt cache, so the stronger tier re-reads the whole context at its
   265	fresh input rate, and a session in which the cheap tier flailed is mostly wrong turns that would anchor the stronger
   266	tier towards the same dead ends. (Added 2026-09-22 at EJ's request; n=0.)
   267	
   268	### 3.6 The rest is a graph
   269	
   270	When the spots are resolved and the clear loops have closed, the algorithm is fully defined:
   271	
   272	- nodes = right-sized pieces (a node may hold its own loop);
   273	- edges = "this piece needs that piece's result";
   274	- gates = a result is verified before anything that depends on it starts;
   275	- a joining node where branches meet.
   276	
   277	Every node carries five things, so that it can be run, checked and resumed without asking anyone:
   278	
   279	| | What it says | Borrowed from Ancient Games |
   280	|---|---|---|
   281	| **Tools** | what the agent uses, each proved working | — |
   282	| **Context** | exactly what the agent is given, and what is withheld | `KNOWN_FACTS`, `READ_SCOPE.deny` |
   283	| **Contract** | the intent, the stop condition, what comes back and in what shape (given to the worker as a template, an example and a standard, 3.8), what may and must not change | `INTENT`, `STOP`, `OUTPUT`, `SCOPE` (`ancient_games/schema.py`) |
   284	| **Evidence** | the proof that comes back with the result and the file it is saved in; a verifier reads the evidence, never the reasoning | `CLAIMS`, `VERIFY_OUTPUT`, `NOT_ESTABLISHED` (the return contract) |
   285	| **State** | what the node reads from the state file and what it writes back | the journal, read before every step |
   286	
   287	An edge is then more than "needs": it is the earlier node's *returns* and *evidence* becoming the later node's context.
   288	
   289	A node that **builds** also names the blueprint sections it depends on and the acceptance criteria it covers
   290	(R1.1, …, and non-functional N1.1, …; ids in exactly that form). Every criterion in scope is covered by at least one
   291	node that builds; a node that does not build carries no criteria, except a blueprint piece, which names the criteria
   292	it is expected to propose. The stop condition of a node that builds has three parts, all objective:
   293	
   294	1. the acceptance criteria it covers pass;
   295	2. what passed before still passes — everything recorded as passing in the **baseline**: the product's test command
   296	   and its output, written to the state file before the first node that builds starts;
   297	3. its contract's "must not change" holds.
   298	
   299	A failure of any of the three is a failed attempt, not a backlog item. If its check is judged, it is a fixed
   300	checklist of those three parts, never an open-ended review. The node `needs` the blueprint piece for any of its
   301	sections that was missing or incomplete, and for any criterion that piece is to propose; it does not start while any
   302	of its sections is unsettled (3.1, blueprint check). Criteria a blueprint piece proposes are fixed when EJ accepts
   303	the piece, before any node that builds starts; if EJ accepts different criteria than the design expected, the nodes
   304	that cover them are planned again, as when a node hits its attempt limit.
   305	
   306	Anything else a worker, a verifier or the final "what is missing" pass finds — a new wish, an improvement, a
   307	problem that neither the criteria in scope nor the baseline covers — is written to the product's
   308	`docs/blueprint/backlog.md` with the date and the run id (a suspected break of something the baseline does not cover
   309	is marked as such, for EJ); it does not become a new piece or a new attempt in this run. This is what lets a run end.
   310	
   311	With everything known, the design's edges decide who works next: a Workflow script, or the COO (the main Claude
   312	session) following the graph. A node's role subagents do not choose the next node. Each node names its role, engine,
   313	model and effort; the COO's contract, and when she does a small node herself, are in `docs/EXECUTOR_KINDS.md`. If a node hits its attempt limit
   314	and its tier exit is spent, it returns to the unclear list and only that part of the graph is planned again.
   315	
   316	Whole picture: graph on the outside, loops inside the nodes, exploration only inside the unknown-spot nodes. This is
   317	what `20260919-state.md` calls combined execution.
   318	
   319	### 3.7 Sequential first; parallel is the last decision
   320	
   321	Design the whole flow as sequential, state-based steps: read the state file → do one step → write the state back.
   322	Only when the design is complete, look for tasks that are **really independent** — all five must hold:
   323	
   324	1. neither needs the other's result;
   325	2. no shared files or resources;
   326	3. doing one would not change how the other is done;
   327	4. each has its own check;
   328	5. the time saved is worth the extra joining step.
   329	
   330	Parallel buys only clock time, and it costs predictability, debuggability and extra joins. Independence of
   331	verifiers is about what they see, not when they run: two verifiers can run one after another and stay independent.
   332	
   333	**Caps (EJ, 2026-10-03; n=0 — a friend's practice plus reasoning, not measured).** At most **5 subagent roles per
   334	run** and at most **3 running at once** (our own number; the code's 3 at `ancient_games/registry.py:24` caps
   335	corroborating dispatches per plan, not concurrency); a retry of the same node is not a new
   336	subagent. A design that needs more is too big: cut it into slices or separate runs, never raise the cap. The
   337	feedback log (3.8 item 11) records calls per run, so the cap is revised from data.
   338	
   339	### 3.8 Running a node: executors are tools, timers, pass-back, no polling
   340	
   341	(Added 2026-10-02 at EJ's request. n=0: every number below is a provisional working value that the ledger replaces
   342	with measured ones. Not yet encoded in `design_gate.py` or `intake.js`.)
   343	
   344	The COO (the main Claude session) is the leader and holds the thinking. Codex, `agy`, `qwen` and role subagents are
   345	**tools** she calls: agents are not yet stable (a call may stop half-way, return nothing, or return something
   346	malformed), and every exchange with them costs the COO a turn that re-reads her whole context. So:
   347	
   348	**Before any of it: know which binary will make the call, and what version it is.** A provider can bundle its own
   349	copy of an engine, older than the copy on `PATH`, and the two do not accept the same model names — the error blames
   350	the model or the account, never the version. Check the catalog of the binary that will actually run, and record that
   351	version in the canary. `docs/EXECUTOR_KINDS.md`, "The bundled-runtime trap", has the three instances that cost this
   352	project three diagnoses in two days.
   353	
   354	1. **One small task per call.** A call has one deliverable, one command line and an expected finish inside its
   355	   timeout. A loop is not inside the call: if a piece loops, the COO or the script runs the loop and each attempt is
   356	   a fresh small call (3.5). A piece too big for one bounded call is not handed over whole; the COO splits it only
   357	   where 3.2's "a label is not a node" allows (own check, different kind, or context room).
   358	2. **Two timers per call, both named in the node.** The *inner* timer is the tool's own (`qwen --max-wall-time`,
   359	   `agy --print-timeout`; `codex exec` has none verified, so it relies on the outer one). The *outer* timer is a
   360	   `timeout` around the whole command, a little longer than the inner. Timeout values scale with the task; the
   361	   provisional sizes are: classification or lookup 60–90 s (the canary's figure), one-file change or one-file test
   362	   set about 5 min, diff review about 5 min, research about 10 min. The design lists them with its cost estimate,
   363	   and the ledger records actual wall time so the numbers get replaced.
   364	3. **No polling.** The COO never repeats "is it done?" and never sleeps in a loop; each check is a turn. She waits
   365	   in one of three ways: a blocking call under the outer timeout; a background run that notifies her when it
   366	   exits; or a small script that waits and prints one line `done|fail|timeout <result path>`. A progress file the
   367	   tool writes is read only after a timeout, to see how far it got. The design states the COO turns it expects per
   368	   node (provisional: two, dispatch and accept) and the total.
   369	4. **Pass-back contract.** Every call writes one result file in the node's own path
   370	   (`runs/<id>/nodes/<node>-<attempt>.result.*`), written to a temp name and renamed, with a fixed header: node,
   371	   attempt, engine, model as the tool reports it, `status: ok|fail|partial`, start and end time, evidence path;
   372	   then the body (`docs/workflow-templates/result-file.md`). A **script** (no model,
   373	   `.claude/skills/workflow-design/scripts/validate_result.py`) validates it: the file exists and is not empty, the
   374	   header parses, the status is present, the reported model, node and attempt equal the contract's, the evidence
   375	   path exists. The COO reads the capped summary (provisional: the
   376	   header and the first 40 lines) and the path, never the raw output.
   377	5. **An unstable return is a failure of the executor, not of the work.** All of these count: no file, empty file,
   378	   unparseable header, wrong model, a timeout, and a clean exit with no result (`qwen` headless exits 0 with no file
   379	   when its write tools are withheld; a wrong `-m` exits 0 and silently runs another model — both seen
   380	   2026-10-02, `docs/EXECUTOR_KINDS.md`). `partial` is a status the COO decides on: retry only the remainder as a
   381	   new small call. Executor failures follow the existing rule: they do not count against the attempt limit, the
   382	   node reruns once fresh on its fallback with a short handoff note, and a second failure blocks it and goes to EJ.
   383	6. **A half-finished node must not leave a mess.** A node that builds writes only inside its contract's "may
   384	   change" paths, and runs from a clean commit or a worktree, so a failed call is discarded whole. The COO never
   385	   reverts files by hand to "tidy up": a revert of anything but a discarded worktree is a Rule 6 confirmation.
   386	7. **Keep the COO's reading small.** Results by path, one-line Log entries, only the state rows the next step
   387	   needs. Raw tool output never enters her context.
   388	8. **The stronger model gives the worker a template, an example and a standard** (EJ, 2026-10-02: this is the
   389	   COO's real work). The COO is the smarter model and the worker is a tool, often a cheaper one; so every brief
   390	   carries (a) the **template** the result must follow, (b) one worked **example** of a good result, small and
   391	   real, and (c) the **standard** it will be judged by: the check as the worker can run it, what failing looks
   392	   like, and the sabotage the check is known to catch. The COO writes these once, in the brief
   393	   (`docs/workflow-templates/node-brief.md`), instead of correcting the result afterwards: it cuts unstable
   394	   returns, retries and her own review reading. A brief with no template, no example or no standard is not
   395	   dispatched; a part she cannot write is an unclear spot (3.3).
   396	9. **Research is written to files.** The *researcher* reads docs, papers and the web (WebSearch, WebFetch). A
   397	   research node writes the full findings to
   398	   `docs/research/<date>-<topic>/FINDINGS.md` (each finding with an id, a source and a label) and returns only a
   399	   digest of about 15 lines, no more than 20, plus the path (`docs/workflow-templates/research-file.md`). The COO
   400	   reads the digest; to settle a doubt she reads one finding by id, or sends a small verify node. A design decided
   401	   from the research goes to `decisions.md` (4).
   402	10. **Exploring existing code is its own role** (EJ, 2026-10-03; n=0). The *explorer* reads the existing system —
   403	   code, tests, configs, git history — never the web. The two roles differ in source, tools, evidence and how a
   404	   claim is checked (`docs/EXECUTOR_KINDS.md`, roles). Its contract:
   405	   - **Read-only**, under a stated budget (tool calls or tokens), favouring recall inside the affected area and
   406	     never a repo-wide overview (TASK_TYPES, case C).
   407	   - **Every claim carries file:line and a short verbatim quote**, and `scripts/quote_check.py` confirms each quote
   408	     is at its line (`docs/workflow-templates/explorer-file.md`); a quote that fails is a failed finding, not a typo.
   409	   - **Every behaviour claim is marked `read` or `ran`.** `read` = inferred from the code; `ran` = confirmed by
   410	     running it (a test, a differential run, `~/skills/verify-extraction`). Only `ran` claims may feed a node that
   411	     builds; a `read` claim a build depends on is an unclear spot until it is run.
   412	   - It returns a digest and the path, like research: the impact map (case C), the old system's behaviours and
   413	     golden outputs (case B), or the findings for use cases #1 and #2.
   414	   - The COO explores herself when the affected area is small and fits her context (whale rule); an explorer node
   415	     is for a large or unfamiliar codebase.
   416	11. **Every run leaves a feedback record** (added 2026-10-02; n=0; `docs/research/20261002-workflow-feedback`).
   417	   During the run, `scripts/runlog.py exec` wraps each Codex, `qwen` or `agy` call (start, end, exit, duration;
   418	   raw output to the gitignored `runs/<id>/raw/`) and `runlog.py verdict` writes one line per node check. After
   419	   it, `scripts/harvest_run.py` reads what the engines already recorded (Workflow run JSON and agent transcripts,
   420	   Codex rollouts joined by time window and cwd, never guessed) into `runs/<id>/feedback.jsonl` and prints a
   421	   digest whose last line is the ledger's Actual cost. Tokens are kept in parts (new input, cache read, output):
   422	   cache reads are often ten times the new input. Only ids, numbers, paths and status are written; the repo is
   423	   public. Every parser fails loudly when an engine changes its format.
   424	12. **Design decisions cite their evidence** (EJ, 2026-10-03; n=0). Model memory goes stale, so every design
   425	   decision cites the finding ids it rests on — research (item 9) or exploration (item 10). A decision with no
   426	   source is marked *model knowledge* and treated like a `read` claim: it may not drive a node that builds until it
   427	   is checked against a source or by running code. The researcher and explorer gather; the COO designs, so their
   428	   picks do not become the design without a second look. Which source comes first depends on the case: the
   429	   codebase for a feature in an existing system (TASK_TYPES case C), current docs for a new stack (case D).
   430	
   431	## 4. State and memory
   432	
   433	The state lives in a file (`docs/workflow-templates/state.md`). Each agent reads it, does one step, writes the new
   434	state back; the next agent, or another tool, continues from there, and a broken run resumes from the last written
   435	state. One writer at a time. The state file holds progress and results, never a worker's reasoning; a worker's
   436	detailed notes go in its own output file, so a verifier can be kept blind to them.
   437	
   438	Kinds of memory when running in Claude Code or Codex with subagents:
   439	
   440	| Memory | Limit |
   441	|---|---|
   442	| Context window | Temporary; a subagent starts with only its prompt; long sessions get summarised |
   443	| `CLAUDE.md` / `AGENTS.md` | Loaded every session, so keep them short and let them point to the rest |
   444	| Claude Code auto-memory | Only Claude Code reads it |
   445	| **Files in the repo** | The one memory every tool and every agent can read — use it for anything that must last |
   446	| Skills | How to do things, not facts about a task |
   447	| Run records (workflow `journal.jsonl`, the Ancient Games journal) | Nobody reads them unless asked |
   448	| A subagent's final report | All that comes back; the rest is lost unless written to a file |
   449	
   450	Working rules: each agent gets a brief with only what its piece needs (with a template, an example and a standard, 3.8) and writes its full result to a file; research and decided designs are files too (3.8, `docs/research/`, `decisions.md`); the
   451	main agent keeps conclusions, not details; stored knowledge carries a date and is rechecked before it is relied on;
   452	knowledge (stable) is kept apart from state (changes every run).
   453	
   454	**Project memory for software work (added 2026-10-02 at EJ's request; n=0).** A release note is written for users
   455	after the fact; it tells an agent what shipped, not why or what to avoid, so it is an output here, not the
   456	memory. A product keeps three small append-only files beside its blueprint, each existing to answer one listed
   457	query (the rule above: no store without a query):
   458	
   459	| File (`docs/blueprint/`) | Answers | Entry (kept to about 5 lines) | Written by, when |
   460	|---|---|---|---|
   461	| `decisions.md` | "Why did we choose X? What did we reject?" | date · decision · why · alternatives rejected · the R/N ids it serves · source (finding ids, or *model knowledge*, 3.8 item 12) | the COO, when a decision spot is answered (3.3) or a design choice is made; EJ's answers are the decisions |
   462	| `changelog.md` | "What did run N change, and did it pass?" | date · run id · what changed · criteria covered (R1.1…) · commits · baseline result · optional `release note:` line for users | the COO, once at the end of each run that builds, from the state file |
   463	| `lessons.md` | "What goes wrong around module Y?" | date · where (path or module) · the trap · how it was found · recheck by (a date or a trigger) | any node proposes, the COO appends; an entry past its recheck is marked stale, never silently trusted |
   464	
   465	Working rules: append only, never rewritten (a reversal is a new entry that points at the old one); every entry
   466	carries a date and, where it applies, a requirement id or a path, so an agent finds it by `grep`, not by reading the
   467	file; readiness (3.1, information) and the design read only the entries for the sections, ids and paths the task
   468	touches; the blueprint's `README.md` map lists the files so an agent knows they exist. A release note, when one is
   469	needed, is generated from the `release note:` lines of the changelog, not written separately. The qwen findings of
   470	2026-10-02 (silent model substitution, exit 0 with no file) are examples of lessons.
   471	
   472	Caution on graphs: this repo built two graphs nobody queried and then deferred a third. Its rule — "no node or edge
   473	type without a listed query" — applies here too (`docs/KNOWLEDGE_GRAPH_DESIGN.md` §1, `docs/STORE_DESIGN_DECISION.md`).
   474	Not verified: Codex's memory and subagent features beyond `AGENTS.md`.
   475	
   476	## 5. Three qualities every design must show
   477	
   478	| Quality | What must be there |
   479	|---|---|
   480	| **Predictability** | The design's edges schedule (script or COO); fixed bounds on attempts, pieces and concurrency; structured outputs; stop conditions and success criteria written before the run — for a product change, the acceptance criteria in scope; a cost estimate before the run |
   481	| **Debuggability** | Every agent labelled; each step's input and output saved to a file; pieces small enough to rerun alone; resume from the failed point; feedback kept per attempt; state in one place |
   482	| **Quality control** | A check per piece; a verifier that does not see the worker's reasoning; proof the check can fail; a contradiction check at joins; a final "what is missing" pass, measured against the three-part stop of 3.6 (a failure there is a failed attempt; anything else it finds goes to the backlog); skipped or unrun checks reported |

