    10	
    11	## 1. The ideas behind it
    12	
    13	- Real tasks are rarely one pattern. Except when a task is tiny, it needs a hybrid. The mix comes from choosing a
    14	  pattern **per piece**, not one pattern for the whole task.
    15	- A piece that is too big fits no single pattern. A piece that is too small costs more than it is worth: every agent
    16	  has a fixed cost, every split loses context, more pieces mean more connections, and a very small piece has
    17	  nothing that can be verified on its own.
    18	- A tiny task needs no workflow, only a prompt file. A workflow is prompts plus the wiring between them.
    19	- Do not assume the model knows what it needs. Every agent starts with only its prompt.
    20	
    21	## 2. When not to use this method
    22	
    23	Understand the challenge first (3.0); the test reads the restatement, not the raw text. If you can state the
    24	restated problem, the fix and the check in one sentence each, and being wrong would show itself at once: write the
    25	prompt file by hand from `docs/workflow-templates/prompt-file.md`, or just do the task. Running the intake
    26	workflow on such a task costs more than the task.
    27	
    28	## 3. The steps
    29	
    30	### 3.0 Understand the challenge — before anything
    31	
    32	(Added 2026-10-01 at EJ's request; extended the same day with the six whys and categorization; not tried on a
    33	real challenge, n=0. Not yet encoded in `intake.js`; see `TODO.md`.)
    34	
    35	From the challenge text alone — no tools, no agents, no repository browsing — state three things:
    36	
    37	- the **objective**: what is true when the task is done;
    38	- **in scope**: what the task touches;
    39	- **out of scope**: what it must not touch.
    40	
    41	Then answer the **six whys**, each feeding one design field. Stop early on any why whose answer no longer
    42	changes the design — six is the ceiling, never a target:
    43	
    44	| # | Why | Feeds | Unanswerable ⇒ |
    45	|---|---|---|---|
    46	| 1 | Why does EJ want this — what problem is behind the ask? | the objective (the XY-problem guard) | unclear spot, decision |
    47	| 2 | Why now — what triggered it? | priority; whether a cheaper fix exists | noted; rarely blocking |
    48	| 3 | Why this shape of solution — why the requested approach over alternatives? | the provisional category and its default pattern (`docs/TASK_TYPES.md`) | decision spot, batched |
    49	| 4 | Why would it fail — what breaks if we get it wrong? | the risk tier → review depth and independent checks | assume the higher tier |
    50	| 5 | Why does it stop where it stops? | in scope / out of scope | decision spot |
    51	| 6 | Why would we believe it is done? | the stop condition and check kind (script, checklist, EJ) | the piece is not loop-ready (3.5) |
    52	
    53	End with a **provisional category** (multi-label, marked *inferred*) from `docs/TASK_TYPES.md`. It is a guess
    54	from the text alone: it feeds the tiny test (§2) and a first default lookup, and 3.2 relabels it from the
    55	algorithm (since 2026-10-02; before, the category was fixed here and only checked against the facts).
    56	
    57	A part that cannot be written, or a challenge that allows two readings, is the first **unclear spot** (kind:
    58	decision, 3.3): a question for EJ with the restatement as the provisional assumption. No new blocking rule is
    59	added: a prompt file's restatement stands on its provisional assumption like any decision, and a design's
    60	question batch reaches EJ before anything runs.
    61	
    62	The tiny test (§2) reads the restatement, not the raw text: tiny means the *restated* problem, fix and check are
    63	one sentence each. A big-sounding challenge with a narrow objective stays small; a vague one ("improve
    64	performance") cannot slip through as tiny.
    65	
    66	The restatement, the why-answers and the provisional category go at the top of the output, beside the verbatim challenge in
    67	the state file, so EJ sees and can correct what the run understood before tokens are spent.
    68	
    69	Cost guard (trial 1, 3.4): this step is one paragraph and six short answers in the same session, never an
    70	agent, never a tool call. On a tiny task it is the prompt file's first lines, so the cheap path stays cheap.
    71	
    72	### 3.1 Readiness — always first
    73	
    74	For the task, and later for each piece, list:
    75	
    76	- **Tools**: the commands, scripts and connections needed, each with the command that proved it works. Run it; do
    77	  not assume. The skill's inventory script (`.claude/skills/workflow-design/scripts/readiness.py`) proves the
    78	  standard set in one pass and marks every line verified / reported / unknown; what it cannot prove stays unknown.
    79	  For a node run by Codex or `agy`, the proof is the canary piece in `docs/EXECUTOR_KINDS.md`, which
    80	  also says which kind a node is assigned to and how its failure shows.
    81	- **Skills** that already cover part of the work.
    82	- **Information**: is the knowledge there; what structure is it in (a state file, a root map, a hierarchy of md
    83	  files, a memory folder, a journal or index, a graph, only the web); and which agents and tools can actually use
    84	  that structure.
    85	
    86	What the information check finds changes the flow:
    87	
    88	| Found | Effect |
    89	|---|---|
    90	| Well-structured files with a map | Fast path: each agent's brief points at exact files |
    91	| There, but unstructured | Add a piece at the front that extracts and organises it |
    92	| Readable by one tool only | Route that piece to that tool, or export what is needed to a plain file |
    93	| There, but not verified | Add a small piece that checks it against the source |
    94	| Missing | Research becomes a piece of the flow; only the pieces that need it wait for it |
    95	
    96	Anything missing becomes a preparation piece at the front. Each task should leave the knowledge better structured
    97	than it found it, so the next task gets the fast path.
