   100	
   101	(Added 2026-09-25 after EJ found that several projects never ended: their workflows built without a fixed target,
   102	so every verify or review step could find more to do. Not yet tried on a real challenge; n=0.)
   103	
   104	A stop condition means something only against a fixed target. For a task that changes a product, that target is the
   105	product's **blueprint**: what the product is for and what it must do, written down and accepted by EJ. The tools and
   106	information checks above do not ask for it, so this check does.
   107	
   108	The blueprint lives in the product's repository, in `docs/blueprint/`, one file per section, with a map
   109	(`docs/blueprint/README.md`) that says where each section is. Where a project already keeps a section elsewhere, the
   110	map points there and that project's rules on who may change it still hold. Layout and headings:
   111	`docs/workflow-templates/blueprint.md`.
   112	
   113	| # | Section | Whose decision |
   114	|---|---|---|
   115	| 1 | Product vision | EJ's. An agent may only write down what EJ has said |
   116	| 2 | Core requirements, each with checkable acceptance criteria (R1, R1.1, …) | EJ's. An agent may draft from the vision |
   117	| 3 | Domain model | drafted by an agent from sections 1–2, accepted by EJ |
   118	| 4 | Business logic | drafted by an agent from sections 2–3, accepted by EJ |
   119	| 5 | System architecture | drafted by an agent from sections 2–4 and 8, accepted by EJ |
   120	| 6 | Data model, schema design and its decisions | drafted by an agent from sections 3–5, accepted by EJ |
   121	| 7 | UI / UX design | drafted by an agent from sections 2–4, accepted by EJ |
   122	| 8 | Non-functional requirements (performance, security, cost, limits) | EJ's. An agent may draft from the vision |
   123	
   124	Each section traces to the sections it is drawn from: a line that serves nothing above it is a finding, not a
   125	feature.
   126	
   127	**Which sections a task needs** depends on what kind of task it is:
   128	
   129	| Kind of task | Sections needed |
   130	|---|---|
   131	| Not a product change (research, a question, an analysis, an edit to documentation only) | none; record why, and the check ends here. It carries no acceptance criteria |
   132	| Fix: restores behaviour the requirements already describe (if they do not describe it, it is a feature) | the requirement it restores, and the sections the fix touches |
   133	| Feature: adds or changes behaviour | vision, requirements, and every section the feature changes |
   134	| New product | all eight present; four settled before anything builds — vision, the first slice's requirements, the hard-to-reverse architecture choices as short decision records, the non-functional targets with runnable checks; the other four settled for the slice only, unknowns marked (EJ, 2026-10-03; was "all eight settled"; n=0) |
   135	
   136	The kind of task is itself checked against the challenge: a task that changes the product's code, schema, UI or
   137	configuration is never "not a product change". A wrong kind switches the whole check off, so it is a stop, not
   138	something a later step can repair.
   139	
   140	**Status of each needed section, judged for this task** (not the status line in the file, which only says whether
   141	EJ accepted it):
   142	
   143	- **settled** — EJ has accepted it (the file says so, with a date) and it covers what this task needs;
   144	- **draft** — it covers what this task needs, but EJ has not accepted it;
   145	- **incomplete** — it exists, accepted or not, but does not cover this task (for example the feature has no
   146	  requirement yet, or the change needs a table the data model does not have);
   147	- **missing** — there is no such section.
   148	
   149	**What each status does to the flow:**
   150	
   151	| Status | Effect |
   152	|---|---|
   153	| settled | nothing; the fast path |
   154	| draft | an unclear spot of kind **decision**: a question for EJ ("accept this section as written?"), the draft being the provisional assumption |
   155	| incomplete | an unclear spot of kind **information**: a blueprint piece at the front drafts the missing part into the section (for requirements: the new R-blocks with their acceptance criteria), and EJ accepts it (the piece's check is EJ's) |
