   108	## Labelling rules (round-1 lessons, 2026-10-02)
   109	
   110	Measured on 50 executor samples (codex 25, agy 25): strict agreement was 12% / 24% before these rules
   111	existed; every disagreement class traced to a missing definition, not to a weak model.
   112	
   113	1. **One label per distinct deliverable — list the deliverables first, then label each.** The primary category
   114	   is the one that answers the ask. Do not add a secondary label for work that is *inside* the primary: a
   115	   research task reads (not also "information extraction"); a debugging diagnosis cites logs (not also
   116	   "research and reports"). Multi-label only when the prompt asks for several *separately delivered* outputs
   117	   ("extract the rules, write them up, then generate test cases" = three deliverables). Dropping a deliverable's
   118	   label is as wrong as adding a passenger label (round-2: combo prompts lost their web-search and
   119	   test-script labels).
   120	2. **Information extraction** applies only when the deliverable *is* the structured data (a table, a schema-
   121	   validated list, field values) — never as a passenger label.
   122	3. **Code review vs research and reports**: review = a verdict on the quality/correctness of work against a
   123	   standard, delivered as an issue report; research = an answer or a findings list. "Compare A to B, list
   124	   divergences with citations" is research; "review this file against the spec, write an issue report" is
   125	   review. **Re-validating an existing finding** against current code ("still open, resolved, or wrong?") is
   126	   research — it updates a findings list, it does not grade work.
   127	4. **Grade a run vs debugging**: grading applies a fixed rubric to a finished run's evidence and stops;
   128	   debugging seeks a root cause. "Which check fired and why" is debugging; "keep the verdicts separate,
   129	   missing proof = UNKNOWN" is grading.
   130	5. **builds=true means the deliverable changes product or test surface** — code, schema, configuration, UI,
   131	   test files, **and generated fixture/seed data that feeds tests or environments** (round-2: fixtures are test
   132	   surface). Findings lists, reports, notes, diaries, command sheets are **not** builds — and neither are
   133	   **deliverable data outputs** (a CSV/JSON table that *is* the answer): a report in data shape is still a
   134	   report (round-3: the gate caught this mislabel on its own corpus author). A `document and explain` piece
   135	   that writes only prose is builds=false even though it writes a file.
   136	6. **tiny + cats=[] is for session-control directives** (status, stop, restate, commit, one-step advice) and
   137	   one-sentence tasks **whose restatement succeeds**: tiny requires the restated problem, fix and check to be
   138	   one sentence each. A prompt you cannot restate ("make it faster", "look into the thing from yesterday") is
   139	   **never tiny** — it is `others` with decision spots. Classifying vague as tiny is the fail-unsafe error: it
   140	   skips the whole method (round-2: the flash tier made exactly this error on 3/3 vague prompts). A standing
   141	   invariant ("do not add fields unless…") is also tiny/[] — it constrains future work, it is not itself a task.
   142	7. **Test cases gen vs test script gen** (round-2, 4 confusions in 50 samples): *cases* = the assertions —
   143	   what behavior is checked, including that content as test code ("extend the unit tests to modules 815–817",
   144	   "add a sabotage case"). *Script* = the machinery that runs things — runners, injectors, drivers, harness
   145	   CLIs, their flags and error reporting ("extend run_e2e.sh with --retry"). If the deliverable would still
   146	   make sense with a different runner, it is cases; if it is about the runner, it is script.
   147	8. **Web search vs research and reports** (round-3): the divider is the *source locus*, not the deliverable
   148	   shape. External sources (the web, vendor docs, "dated sources" from outside) → `web search`, even when the
   149	   output is a report. Material inside the repo → `research and reports`.
   150	9. **Document and explain vs research and reports / code generation** (round-3): when the deliverable is prose
   151	   for a human audience, it is `document and explain` — even when sources must be read first, and even when the
   152	   prose lands in a knowledge file of the repo (writing rules into EXECUTOR_KINDS.md is document and explain,
   153	   not code generation). Research answers a question; a document is made to be read.
   154	
   155	## Combination pipelines (seeded)
   156	
   157	| Pipeline | Nodes (in order) | Loop sits at | Join check |
   158	|---|---|---|---|
   159	| research → codegen | research, codegen | codegen node | research answered its questions (checklist); else codegen does not start |
   160	| codegen → tests → review | codegen, tests gen, review | codegen node | review checklist against the acceptance criteria |
   161	| debugging | repro, fix, rerun | the whole piece | the repro test |
   162	| scan → report | scan sections, report join | none | coverage manifest complete; contradictions = stop |
   163	| grade → fix → regrade | grade, fix, regrade | the fix node | the regrade verdict on the same rubric; a FAIL surviving the regrade goes to EJ |
   164	
   165	New combinations earn a row here only after the ledger shows the ad-hoc version ran twice.
   166	
   167	### Candidate pipelines (not rows yet — each earns its row after two real runs in the ledger)
   168	
   169	**build a feature (use case #4)** — EJ, 2026-10-02; n=0.
   170	
   171	1. **Research — the main agent (COO) herself**, with a research skill, writing findings and a digest to
   172	   `docs/research/` (method 3.8 item 9). Research agents only when there are many main sources (context room) or a
   173	   source needs a different kind (web versus repo).
   174	2. **Design and small examples — the COO.** The design, the acceptance criteria in scope, and the briefs for the
   175	   next two nodes, each with a template, a worked example and the standard (method 3.8 item 8).
   176	3. **Dev ∥ verifier phase 1, in parallel** (passes the five tests of method 3.7: one shared input, different
   177	   deliverables, separate paths).
   178	   - *Dev* (the row's coder, Codex `gpt-6.1-sol`): implements and writes **unit tests only**.
   179	   - *Verifier, phase 1* (a different kind from the dev, EXECUTOR_KINDS rule 5): writes the test plan and the
   180	     acceptance cases from the design and acceptance criteria only, **before it has seen any code**. Its cases are
   181	     first run against a deliberately broken build and must fail there (sabotage), or they are not used.
   182	4. **Script gate** (no model; `.claude/skills/workflow-design/scripts/feature_gate.py` — `baseline` from two
   183	   JUnit runs of the full suite before anything builds, `check` against the COO's `plan.json` of may-change,
   184	   must-not-change and protected globs): the dev's new unit tests pass; the per-test baseline still passes; every file in the
   185	   diff is planned or has a listed reason (scope); nothing in "must not change" changed; the verifier's cases and
   186	   any goldens are untouched (tamper guard). A failure goes straight back to the dev; it costs no review.
   187	5. **Verifier, phase 2** — the same verifier, its cases already fixed, so seeing the code cannot bend them: reviews
   188	   the diff (quality, conventions, design fit, security, scope) and runs its cases. One result file, its findings
   189	   split into *behaviour* (a case failed) and *review* (a code issue).
   190	6. **Findings → the COO.** A small issue (her small-node conditions and 8-tool-call cap,
   191	   `docs/EXECUTOR_KINDS.md`) she fixes herself, quickly, and the verifier reruns. A big issue goes back to the dev,
   192	   at most **2 rounds**, then the tier exit or EJ. Findings outside the acceptance criteria go to the backlog, not
   193	   into this run.
   194	
   195	The COO does not review (EJ, 2026-10-03, from a friend's practice): she plans, decides, monitors and makes quick
   196	fixes; the mechanical part of review is the script gate and the judgment part is the verifier. Roles per run:
   197	researcher or explorer, dev, verifier — 3 or 4, inside the cap of 5 (method 3.7).
   198	
   199	Join check: the verifier's cases (proven able to fail) pass, the baseline still passes, "must not change" holds —
   200	the three-part stop of method 3.6 — and the verifier's review has no open finding inside the criteria. Feedback:
   201	`runlog.py` wraps the dev and verifier calls and logs each verdict, gate included (method 3.8 item 11).
   202	
   203	**Lead-in by case (EJ, 2026-10-03; restructured the same day from `docs/research/20261003-feature-cases/`,
   204	critic's keep/shrink list adopted; n=0 — candidate text, none becomes a rule before two real runs).** Steps 3–6
   205	above are the same in every case. Two questions, in this order, pick the lead-in:
   206	
   207	1. **Are the requirements clear?** No → case **A** first, then the second question.
   208	2. **What system exists?** An old one being replaced → **B**; an existing one getting a feature → **C**; none →
   209	   **D**. A migration whose target does not exist yet is **B + D**.
   210	
   211	Hand-off: **B and D end as C** — their last step records the per-test baseline, so the next feature on that system
   212	is case C. A run names its case(s) in the restatement (method 3.0) and checks them against the facts at readiness:
   213	an existing codebase is never D, and a B run that cannot run the old system is not ready.
   214	
   215	**Shared in every case.**
   216	
   217	1. Every acceptance criterion carries at least one concrete example (input → expected). The blind verifier works from
   218	   it. (A new rule: neither the method nor the blueprint template asked for examples before.)
   219	2. Every dev and verifier brief has a stop-on-unclear clause: return `UNCLEAR: <question> / <best guess>` instead of
   220	   guessing.
   221	3. Conventions and invariants are executable checks (lint, tests, hooks) wherever possible; the context file
   222	   (`CLAUDE.md`, `AGENTS.md`) is short and hand-written; no agent-written overview file.
   223	4. Tamper guard: golden files and the verifier's cases are read-only to the dev; the join fails if a case disappears
   224	   or the case count drops.
   225	5. Scope check in the script gate: every file in the diff is planned, or has a listed reason.
   226	6. Where code exists, the baseline is the full suite recorded per test id before anything builds (run twice; a
   227	   test that flips is listed as flaky, not as a failure).
   228	7. Requirements are settled per slice: a very unclear or large feature is cut into thin end-to-end slices, each
   229	   run through the pipeline.
   230	8. Behavioural equivalence and regressions are judged only by running code, never by an LLM reading it.
   231	
   232	| Case | Source of truth | Lead-in (before anything builds) | Verifier's oracle | Main risk |
   233	|---|---|---|---|---|
   234	| A. Requirements not clear | EJ, through questions | "Not now" is a valid exit of the restatement (method 3.0). At most 5 questions, ranked by impact × uncertainty, each multiple-choice or answerable in a few words, with a recommended answer; anything findable in the code is research, not a question. Everything not asked goes on an assumptions list that EJ accepts with the R-blocks; the questions and answers are logged in `decisions.md`. A throwaway prototype only for a question EJ cannot answer in words; EJ accepts the list of requirements taken from it, never the prototype. Nothing builds until the slice's requirements are settled. | The criteria EJ accepted, with their examples | Building the wrong thing |
   235	| B. Migration from an old system | The old system's recorded behaviour | One port / drop / change list of the old behaviours; EJ marks each, and intended differences are tracked there. The oracle is the old system's recorded output (golden input → output pairs, or old-versus-new runs), never the extracted description. Readiness: the old system can be run to record outputs. The verifier aims golden inputs at the measured bug classes (types and precision, parsing and formatting, boundaries, error paths). Only when old-versus-old runs differ: a noise ignore list with reasons. Only when data moves: schema, then counts per column, then a hash per row. Small pieces in dependency order. | Parity on the goldens, plus the data checks when data moves | A plausible but wrong description of the old system; old bug workarounds ported as requirements |
   236	| C. New feature in an existing system | The existing code plus the new requirement | The scan is systematic inside the affected area, under a budget, favouring recall; never a repo-wide overview. It produces a 4-item impact map with file:line: where the change goes; what calls it and what it calls; the nearby conventions; what must not change. Load-bearing behaviour claims are checked by running code (shared 8). Characterization tests only for "must not change" entries that have no test; they join the baseline. In phase 1 the verifier gets only the "must not change" list from the map. Language and conventions are read, not asked. | The new criteria, plus the per-test baseline still passing | Breaking something not known to be connected; agents going beyond the request |
   237	| D. Feature for a new system | EJ's blueprint | Stack and standards questions (below); a mainstream stack, its reason in `decisions.md`. Blueprint: all eight sections present, four settled before scaffolding (method 3.1). A walking-skeleton node before dev ∥ verifier: lint, tests, one end-to-end test, and a deliberate layer break the checks must catch. A few structural checks: a secret scan, an authorization test if there is auth, one layer rule. | The criteria, the skeleton's end-to-end test and the structural checks (no behavioural baseline yet) | Architecture fixed too early or too vaguely; agents writing everything in one file |
   238	
   239	Deferred (evidence too thin or not EJ's single-developer case): shadow or dark launch (B); a repo map or code graph
   240	(C); a larger retry budget for mechanical translation (it conflicts with the 2-round cap); a duplication limit and a
   241	security rescan after every fix round (D).
   242	
   243	**Proof of concept (POC) — when and how (EJ, 2026-10-03; n=0).** A POC answers one technical risk that nobody
   244	can settle by reading or asking (will this library do X, is it fast enough, does this API behave as documented).
   245	It is method 3.3's bounded explore loop for a technical unknown; the age of the system is not the reason for it.
   246	
   247	- **When, by case.** D: usually, for the riskiest unknown before the stack is locked. B: never for behaviour (the
   248	  old system defines it); only for a risky part of the target platform (volume, one tricky conversion). C: only
   249	  when the feature brings in something the codebase has never used. A: a different kind — a throwaway prototype
   250	  for EJ to decide behaviour or UI, then deleted.
   251	- **Shape.** One named question; a pass/fail answer written before it starts; a time or token limit; it ends
   252	  deleted, or explicitly promoted into the walking skeleton. A throwaway POC is not the skeleton.
   253	- **Roles.** The researcher proposes the candidate approaches from current sources; a coder (cheap tier)
   254	  builds each POC; a script checks each against the pass/fail written first; the COO decides. The researcher
   255	  does not build POCs: reading and building need different tools and different evidence.
   256	- **Strategy.** Round 1: list the genuinely different approaches (library A versus library B versus hand-written,
   257	  or different engines — not copies: one model asked for many candidates converges on two or three ideas) and run
   258	  **at most 3 in parallel** (independent: separate folders, own check; method 3.7). All get the same check and
   259	  measurement, written before any starts; the decision follows the written criteria, not impression. None passes →
   260	  round 2 with what was learned, again at most 3; after 2 rounds → EJ. **Up to 5** in a round only when the check
   261	  is fully automatic and the approaches are truly different; never 10. The winner is deleted or promoted; each
   262	  loser gets one line in `decisions.md` saying why it lost.
   263	- **Why 3.** Building candidates is cheap; judging them is the bottleneck (the COO's context, EJ's attention).
   264	  It matches the repo's anchors: Gate splits above 3 (`ancient_games/stages.py:109`) and the plan-wide cap of 3
   265	  corroborating dispatches (`ancient_games/registry.py:24` — not a concurrency cap). Reasoning, not measurement: the feedback log records per round how many
   266	  candidates ran and which round produced the winner, and the number is revised from that.
   267	
   268	**Stack and standards questions — whenever the target is new (EJ, 2026-10-03; n=0).**
   269	
   270	- **Target is new** (D, and B when the target system does not exist yet — a "B + D" run, where the old system
   271	  says *what* and these questions decide *how*): ask the language and framework, coding standard and lint, test
