12afdec 2026-09-20 Workflow design method, templates, and the intake workflow
     1	---
     2	name: workflow-design
     3	description: "Designing a workflow for an incoming task or challenge — or deciding that none is needed — before any agents are dispatched: understand the challenge (objective, scope, six whys, category and its default pattern), check readiness (including the product blueprint: vision, requirements with acceptance criteria, domain, architecture, data, UI, non-functional), write the algorithm first, list every unclear spot, size the pieces, run clear pieces as loops with objective checks, then wire the rest as a graph whose nodes each carry tools, context, a contract, evidence and state; sequential and state-file driven first, parallel decided last. Use whenever someone asks to design, plan, create, improve or review a workflow, an agent pipeline, an orchestration or a multi-agent / subagent setup; asks how many agents a task needs, whether to parallelise or loop, or how agents should pass information; brings a task too big for one prompt; or when you are about to write a Workflow script or dispatch several subagents without a written design. NOT for: a task of one to three obvious steps (just do it), the Workflow script API (use workflow-authoring), or running an already designed workflow."
     4	---
     5	
     6	# Workflow design
     7	
     8	The job: turn a challenge into **a prompt file** (small task) or **a workflow design** (bigger task). You are
     9	designing, not executing. The full method with its reasons is `references/method.md`; the five templates are in
    10	`assets/templates/`. Read the method once before your first design in a session.
    11	
    12	Why this exists: agents left alone pick a pattern first ("let's fan out five subagents") and discover later that
    13	they lacked information, that a loop had no way to close, or that two parallel workers collided. This method makes
    14	the cheap discoveries first.
    15	
    16	## First: understand the challenge (method 3.0)
    17	
    18	From the challenge text alone — no tools, no agents. State the **objective** (what is true when the task is
    19	done), what is **in scope**, what is **out of scope**. Then the **six whys**, each feeding one design field:
    20	why does the person want this (objective) · why now (priority) · why this shape of solution (category + default
    21	pattern) · why would it fail (risk tier → review depth) · why does it stop where it stops (scope) · why would
    22	we believe it is done (check kind). Stop early when an answer no longer changes the design — six is a ceiling,
    23	never a target. End with a **provisional category** (multi-label, marked inferred) from `references/task-types.md`: a guess
    24	that feeds the tiny test and a first default lookup; step 2 relabels it from the algorithm. A part that cannot be written, or a challenge that allows two readings, is the first unclear
    25	spot (kind: decision) — a question for the person with the restatement as the provisional assumption. The
    26	restatement goes at the top of the output, beside the verbatim challenge in the state file. One paragraph in
    27	the same session, never an agent, never a tool call. (Added 2026-10-01 at EJ's request; six whys and
    28	categorization the same day; n=0.)
    29	
    30	## Then: is it tiny?
    31	
    32	The test reads the restatement, not the raw text. If the restated problem, the fix and the check are one sentence
    33	each, and a mistake would show itself at once, do not design a workflow. Do the task, or fill in
    34	`assets/templates/prompt-file.md`. A process that costs more than the task is a failure of the method, not a
    35	success. (Trial 1, n=1: a one-line README fix went through the full process and cost about 309k tokens, partly
    36	because checks and handover were counted as steps. Count only the steps the challenge itself needs.)
    37	
    38	## The steps
   145	## Every node carries five things
   146	
   147	| | What to write |
   148	|---|---|
   149	| **Tools** | what the agent uses, each proved working |
   150	| **Context** | exactly what it is given, and what is withheld (a verifier never sees the worker's reasoning) |
   151	| **Contract** | the intent, the observable stop condition (for a piece that builds: its acceptance criteria pass, the baseline still passes, "must not change" holds), what it returns and in what shape, what it may and must not change |
   152	| **Evidence** | the proof that comes back with the result (a command and its output, or file:line) and the file it is saved in |
   153	| **State** | what it reads from the state file before starting and what it writes back |
   154	
   155	An edge is then concrete: the earlier node's returns and evidence become the later node's context.
   156	
   157	## State file
   158	
   159	The state lives in a file (`assets/templates/state.md`): each agent reads it, does one step, writes the new state
   160	back. One writer at a time. It holds progress and results, never reasoning — notes go in the worker's own output
   161	file. This is what lets another agent, another tool, or tomorrow's session continue the run.
   162	
   163	## Before calling a design finished
   164	
   165	Check it shows all three, and say so in the design:
   166	
   167	- **Predictability** — the design's edges schedule (script or COO); limits on attempts, pieces and concurrency; structured outputs; success
   168	  criteria written before the run (for a product change: the acceptance criteria in scope); an agent count and a
   169	  cost estimate with its basis.
   170	- **Debuggability** — labelled agents; every step's input and output in a file; pieces small enough to rerun alone;
   171	  a resume point after a failure.
   172	- **Quality control** — a check per piece; blind verifiers; proof each script check can fail; a contradiction check
   173	  at joins; a final "what is missing" pass measured against the three-part stop of step 8 (a failure there is a
   174	  failed attempt; anything else goes to the backlog); skipped or unrun checks reported as such.
   175	- **Engine split** — for build work, run `scripts/workload.py --plan <plan> --write` before the run starts. It gives **opencode priority** (EJ, 2026-10-04) by **module**, crossing each module's code and tests so the tests are written by the engine that did not write the code. The default is the *smallest* lead that counts as priority — which at five modules is exactly 60/40, costing one module its independent tests; demanding 60% on a larger plan costs far more, so `--share` is there when the bigger lead is wanted. Never split dev and tests independently: that gives half the modules their code *and* their tests from one engine. `check()` refuses a plan where opencode does not lead, that self-tests more than its lead costs, or whose verifier shares its node's engine. `docs/EXECUTOR_KINDS.md` has the rule and the arithmetic.
   176	
   177	## Output
   178	
   179	- Small task → `assets/templates/prompt-file.md`, filled in.
   180	- Bigger task → `assets/templates/workflow-design.md`, filled in, with `state.md` and `readiness.md` beside it.
   181	- A blueprint piece writes into the product's `docs/blueprint/`, following `assets/templates/blueprint.md`.
   182	- Put a run's files in one folder (in the Ancient Games repo: `runs/<YYYYMMDD-slug>/`).
   183	- Give the person the questions batch and the cost estimate before anything is run. Designing is cheap; running
   184	  agents is not, so a run needs their go-ahead.
   185	
   186	## Running the method as a script
   187	
   188	`~/dev/ancient-games/.claude/workflows/intake.js` walks a challenge through steps 1–4 with one agent per step and
   189	fixed yes/no checks in script code (args `{challenge, runId}`). It is a multi-agent run — about 300k tokens in its
   190	one trial — so use it only when the person asks for it. Its offline test: `node tests/workflows/intake_harness.mjs`.
   191	
   192	## Related skills
   193	
   194	`challenge-mediation` types a challenge and rates the cost of being wrong — useful input to the risk judgment in
   195	step 4. `task-decomposition-strategies` helps with splitting; where it leans parallel, this method's sequential-first
   196	rule wins. `agent-loop` enforces a test loop for step 5. `chronos-ledger` tracks state across sessions.
   197	`workflow-authoring` is the script API for turning a finished design into a Workflow script.
   198	
   199	Everything here comes from one working session and one trial (n=1); the restate step and the tier rule in step 5
   200	have had none (n=0). Treat the numbers — the 3-step rule, the cost figures — as working values to be tested, and
   201	tell the person when a real task contradicts them.
    29	  U --> S3["3 LIST EVERY SPOT BEFORE RESOLVING ANY, 3.3<br/>kind, what it blocks, what it depends on.<br/>listing first matters: one answer often<br/>removes another spot"]
